<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
	xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
	package="wootbeer.gotandroid"
	android:installLocation="auto">

	<application
		android:allowBackup="false"
		android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
		android:label="@string/app_name"
		android:appCategory="game"
		tools:targetApi="o">
		<!--
			wootbeer: "if during the boss battle I lock the android device's screen, then come back
			to the game, the game's music actually changed." Root cause: this activity was
			missing android:configChanges entirely, and android:screenOrientation below is
			"sensorLandscape", a device orientation SENSOR reading, not a fixed rotation,
			which keeps reporting a value even face-down/locked in a pocket, and very often
			reports a DIFFERENT landscape reading (regular vs. upside-down landscape) by the
			time the screen is unlocked than it did going in. Without configChanges listing
			"orientation" (and, per Android's own docs, "screenSize"/"screenLayout" alongside it
			on API 13+, since a sensor-driven landscape flip can also touch those), Android's
			default behavior for ANY of those config deltas, even ones that never change this
			fixed-landscape layout at all, is to fully DESTROY AND RECREATE the Activity
			(onCreate() again), which builds a brand-new GotView instance. That new instance's
			own gotRunning starts false, so its surfaceCreated() (GotView.java) takes the
			cold-start branch and spawns a SECOND native gotMain() (got_main.c) call on a new
			thread, while the ORIGINAL render thread (still correctly blocked inside
			pauseRenderThread(), holding the real in-progress boss fight) is simply orphaned,
			never torn down. This port's own native globals (s_boss_active, s_current_level,
			etc.) are plain C statics in the one shared library instance for this process, so
			they're untouched by the new Activity, the boss fight's own game STATE survives
			intact, but gotMain()'s own one-time startup sequence unconditionally calls
			got_play_music(MUSIC_OPENSONG, false) once GOTRES.DAT loads successfully (see that
			call site's own comment: correct for an actual cold launch, since nothing else could
			already be playing at that exact point, not true here), stomping the boss music
			that was still legitimately active in the orphaned original session. Fixed the
			standard Android way: android:configChanges below tells the OS this activity handles
			these config deltas itself (a no-op here, since onConfigurationChanged() is never
			overridden and the locked sensorLandscape layout never actually needs to change),
			so Android now leaves the existing Activity/GotView instance (and its one real render
			thread/gotMain() call) alone across an orientation-sensor reading change instead of
			recreating it, letting the already-correct onPause()/onResume() plus
			Want_pause/pauseRenderThread() handshake (section 31 area) do what it already does
			right: block during the lock, resume the SAME boss track exactly where it left off.
		-->
		<activity
			android:name=".GotActivity"
			android:label="@string/app_name"
			android:launchMode="singleTask"
			android:screenOrientation="sensorLandscape"
			android:theme="@style/GotTheme"
			android:windowSoftInputMode="adjustNothing"
			android:hardwareAccelerated="false"
			android:exported="true"
			android:configChanges="orientation|screenSize|screenLayout|keyboardHidden">
			<intent-filter>
				<action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
				<category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
			</intent-filter>
		</activity>
	</application>
</manifest>
